EXT. JUNKYARD - BURNED-OUT SCHOOL BUS CLUBHOUSE - LATE AFTERNOON

Summer, 1974. Heat shimmers over a hill of stripped sedans. Inside the long skeleton of a yellow bus, someone has laid down carpet scraps, strung Christmas lights, and nailed a Beatles poster to the ceiling. The seats have been pulled out and replaced with milk crates.

TEDDY LOWELL, 15, a girl with scabbed knees and a pencil behind her ear, sprawls on a crate with a notebook in her lap. PEDRO SALAZAR, 16, lean and quiet, tinkers with a transistor radio. RUTHIE KWAN, 14, perched on the driver's seat, spins the big steering wheel with one finger. DANNY FITZGERALD, 17, the oldest, leans in the doorway, thumbs hooked in his belt loops, a pack of cigarettes just visible in his sock.

The radio crackles and settles on a pop song. They all pause to listen.

TEDDY
Okay. Official meeting of the Wheel Club. Everybody sit.

RUTHIE
I'm already sitting.

TEDDY
Everybody who isn't Ruthie, sit.

DANNY
I'll stand.

TEDDY
You always stand.

DANNY
That's why I'm taller.

PEDRO
It's because he can't fit on the crates.

DANNY
Say that again.

PEDRO
You can't fit on the crates.

Danny laughs and ruffles his hair. Pedro ducks, grinning.

TEDDY
Item one. The summer plan.

RUTHIE
We have a plan?

TEDDY
We have a list.

RUTHIE
Read it.

TEDDY
One. Paint the bus. Two. Get the radio working. Three. Finish the map of the junkyard so nobody gets lost. Four. Never tell anyone about the bus.

PEDRO
Five. Eat.

TEDDY
Five. Eat.

DANNY
Six. Don't get arrested.

TEDDY
That one's implied.

RUTHIE
What about the thing in the back?

The room goes quiet. The radio hisses between stations. Danny glances at the door. Pedro puts down his screwdriver.

TEDDY
Which thing?

RUTHIE
You know which thing.

TEDDY
We said we weren't going to talk about it.

RUTHIE
You said. I didn't say.

PEDRO
Ruthie.

RUTHIE
It's been there since June. Someone has to say it.

DANNY
It's a trunk. It's an old trunk.

RUTHIE
It has a lock.

DANNY
Lots of trunks have locks.

RUTHIE
It has a lock and it's NEW.

PEDRO
Who put a new lock on an old trunk?

Nobody answers. Teddy closes her notebook with a small, careful click. She looks around the bus as if seeing it for the first time, her eyes catching on each face before settling on Danny.

TEDDY
Danny.

DANNY
What?

TEDDY
You've been here more than anyone.

DANNY
It's my brother's yard. I'm supposed to be here.

TEDDY
Is the trunk yours?

DANNY
No.

TEDDY
Is it your brother's?

DANNY
Teddy.

TEDDY
Is it?

DANNY
I don't know what's in it.

TEDDY
That isn't what I asked.

He crosses the bus in three long strides and drops to a crouch beside the trunk, his back to them. His hand rests on the lock. Pedro stands. Ruthie slides down from the driver's seat. For a moment nobody breathes.

DANNY
(not turning)
He asked me to keep it safe. That's all he said. Keep it safe, don't open it, don't tell anyone.

TEDDY
And you told us.

DANNY
I didn't tell you. You found out.

TEDDY
Because you left the key where Ruthie could find it.

DANNY
I never did.

RUTHIE
(very small)
It was under the radio.

He turns, finally. His face, that easy older-brother face, has gone grey as the road. He looks at Ruthie, at the key in her palm, at the three of them standing close together in the strung-up lights.

DANNY
Give me the key.

RUTHIE
No.

DANNY
Ruthie--

RUTHIE
We're the Wheel Club. We decide together.

PEDRO
(quietly)
She's right.

TEDDY
Open it, Ruthie.

Ruthie steps forward, fits the key, turns it. The lock pops. The lid swings up on rusted hinges and a hush falls over the bus as the afternoon light finds what is inside: not money, not guns, not anything they imagined, but row upon row of school photographs, hundreds of them, each of a child they know from town, each with a name written on the back in careful pencil, and on top, a single Polaroid of the four of them, taken that very morning, through the bus window, from outside.
